    definition: "Lifecycle assessment (LCA) systematically evaluates environmental impacts of products throughout complete lifecycles: raw material extraction and processing, manufacturing and assembly, transportation and distribution, product use and maintenance, and end-of-life disposal or recycling. LCA prevents optimising one lifecycle phase whilst overlooking impacts in others—for example, lightweight materials might reduce use-phase emissions but create extraction impacts.",
    whyItMatters: "LCA methodology appears in HSC environmental analysis questions and within design evaluation. Understanding that environmental impact isn't determined by single factors but emerges from complete lifecycle analysis prevents naive conclusions. For example, \"recyclable materials are automatically sustainable\" requires LCA investigation—if recycling infrastructure doesn't exist regionally, recyclable materials become landfill. LCA develops systems thinking beyond surface-level environmental claims.",
    scenario: "A student's design incorporated biodegradable materials, assuming this was automatically environmentally superior. Lifecycle assessment revealed: growing corn for biopolymers required pesticides and land use (environmental cost), manufacturing required energy-intensive fermentation (carbon footprint), shipping bioplastics required transportation energy, and \"biodegradable\" only occurred in industrial composting facilities unavailable locally (consumers' bioplastics became landfill alongside conventional plastic). Full lifecycle analysis revealed this option wasn't unambiguously superior to well-established recycling systems.",
