    definition: "Quality assurance involves systematic processes ensuring designs meet intended standards and specifications. QA includes documentation of quality standards (what must be achieved), inspection procedures (how compliance is verified), corrective actions (how shortcomings are addressed), and final acceptance criteria (determining when products are complete and acceptable). Quality assurance differs from quality control—QA is preventative (setting standards before production) whilst QC is corrective (finding problems after production).",
    whyItMatters: "The Major Design Project assessment values quality awareness. Rather than hoping prototypes work out acceptably, demonstrating systematic quality assurance shows professional design discipline. This includes: developing explicit quality standards for your prototype (what constitutes acceptable dimensions, surface finish, structural integrity), inspecting against these standards during production, and documenting any quality issues and their resolution. This demonstrates production maturity beyond simply building something that \"looks okay.\"",
    scenario: "A student completed prototype fabrication without formal quality assurance. Upon informal examination, they discovered dimensional errors (some components varied by 3mm from specification), surface finish inconsistencies (variable sanding quality across surfaces), and structural concerns (some fasteners weren't torqued to specification). Rework consumed several weeks. A peer who implemented quality assurance procedures (measuring components immediately after fabrication, sanding to specification with documented methods, torquing fasteners to documented values) discovered and addressed minor issues immediately, preventing late-stage catastrophes.",
    example: "I implemented quality assurance procedures including: documented standards (all components must be within ±0.5mm specification, surface finish must be smooth with no visible defects, fasteners must be torqued to 3.5Nm specification), inspection schedule (measuring each component upon completion before assembly), corrective actions (refinishing surfaces failing initial inspection standards, re-machining components exceeding tolerance before assembly), and final acceptance criteria (complete prototype must pass drop test, load test, and 50-cycle durability test before declaring completion). Documentation showing which items failed inspection and corrective actions demonstrated quality discipline.\"\n\n---",
